---
<p>Each feature exists in other languages; the combination does not. Every example is a complete program that compiles and runs. Features in the specification but not the compiler are marked "planned" and shown as fragments.</p>

<h2 id="particles">Particles: argument roles in the name</h2>
<p>A required parameter after the first carries a role word: <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code> or <code>as</code>; the call writes the same word. The particle is part of the name: <code>transfer(from:to:)</code>.</p>
<example name="features-particles"/>
<p>Swapping the accounts swaps the labels, so the mistake shows in the text. A particle appears at most once per signature; a <code>bool</code> argument must be named.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors as values: fails, try, catch</h2>
<p>A function lists its errors with <code>fails</code>:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Written</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>fail .x</code></td><td>Leaves with an error.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>try f()</code></td><td>Passes a failure up.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>f() catch value</code></td><td>Uses a fallback.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>f() catch { … }</code></td><td>Runs a block that must leave: <code>return</code>, <code>break</code> or <code>continue</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>f() catch switch { … }</code></td><td>Matches the error exhaustively.</td></tr>
</table>
<example name="features-errors"/>
<p>Errors compose (<code>union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError</code>) and narrow (<code>union error Retryable = CarrierError except .rejected</code>); see the <a href="errors.html">Errors guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="defer-failing">defer failing</h2>
<p><code>defer</code> runs a statement at scope end, <code>defer failing</code> only when the function leaves by failing, for rollback. Deferred statements run in reverse and cannot fail.</p>
<example name="features-defer"/>
<p>On the failing path <code>rollback</code> prints before <code>always</code>: the later <code>defer</code> runs first.</p>

<h2 id="effects">Effects: uses</h2>
<p>A public function declares what it does besides computing. <code>checksum</code> has no <code>uses</code> clause, so it is pure: no allocation, no I/O. <code>squares</code> allocates, and says so.</p>
<example name="features-effects"/>
<p>Omitting <code>uses alloc</code> is a compile error (BYN6002). Effects: <code>alloc suspend net fs clock random env process console</code>. Replacing a handler with <code>provide</code> is planned, so a network test needs no network: <code>provide net: FakeNet(…) { … }</code>. <code>provide allocator:</code> works today.</p>

<h2 id="units">Units</h2>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> The specification has units as types with literal suffixes; the compiler does not parse <code>unit</code> declarations yet.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unit Seconds(s): i64
unit Millis(ms) = Seconds / 1000

let t = 2s + 500ms        // Millis 2500: conversion goes to the finer unit
let whole = t.floor&lt;Seconds&gt;()   // going coarser names its rounding</code></pre>
<p>Mixing units is a compile error. A bare <code>0</code> never becomes a unit value; write <code>0g</code>. Units are erased at run time.</p>

<h2 id="untrusted">Untrusted&lt;T&gt;</h2>
<p>A value from outside can be wrapped as <code>Untrusted&lt;T&gt;</code>, with no implicit conversion either way; unwrapping needs <code>trustUnchecked()</code> plus <code>because</code> and a written reason.</p>
<example name="features-untrusted"/>
<p>The specification adds sinks such as file paths, redirect URLs and process arguments, which reject <code>Untrusted</code> values, and only a type constructed with <code>requires</code> clears the taint. Request and input sources are not wrapped yet: planned.</p>

<h2 id="states">Struct states</h2>
<p>A struct can declare a lifecycle in <code>states</code>; <code>Order.paid</code> is then a type. A method names the state it needs (<code>ref this is .paid</code>) and the one it leaves (<code>becomes .shipped</code>).</p>
<example name="features-states"/>
<p>Calling <code>ship</code> on a draft is a compile error. A state's payload (<code>payment</code>, <code>tracking</code>) is readable only where the type guarantees the state. States cost nothing at run time: checks happen at compile time.</p>

<h2 id="requires">requires</h2>
<p><code>requires</code> on a type or field is checked at construction: a <code>Box</code> that breaks its rules cannot exist. In a clause, <code>it</code> is the value checked; a rule naming other fields belongs to the struct, and <code>at .w</code> names the field a violation points at.</p>
<example name="features-requires"/>
<p>Building such a type is fallible: it needs <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>, and the error is <code>ValidationError&lt;Box&gt;</code>. Updates go through <code>with</code>, which checks again. Function preconditions are <code>expects</code>, which panics on a violation in every build mode.</p>

<h2 id="because">@attr because "…"</h2>
<p>Anywhere code bypasses a safety rule, a written reason is required. A missing <code>because</code> is a compile error (BYN8001). <code>bayan audit &lt;path&gt;</code> lists each bypass with its reason, today only the <code>trustUnchecked() because</code> ones.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Form</th><th>Today</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>@allow(BYN5102) because "…"</code></td><td>Planned</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>x.trustUnchecked() because "…"</code></td><td>Works</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>unsafe because "…" { … }</code></td><td>Planned</td></tr>
</table>
<pre><code class="byn">let name = x.trustUnchecked() because "checked by the caller"

unsafe because "the C API wants a raw pointer" {
    …
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="postfix-if">Statement if suffixes</h2>
<p>A simple statement can end with <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code>, on the same line and evaluated first; the statement runs only if it applies. Allowed on <code>return</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, <code>panic</code>, a single call and a single assignment, not blocks or chains.</p>
<example name="features-postfix"/>

<h2 id="ref-this">ref this: mutation in the signature</h2>
<p>A method is read-only unless its first parameter is <code>ref this</code>. A changing method cannot be called on a <code>let</code>, and the signature says which methods change the value.</p>
<example name="reading-ref-this"/>

<h2 id="context-names">.member and it: names the compiler can supply</h2>
<p>A leading dot names a member of the expected type: <code>.red</code> for <code>Color.red</code>, <code>fail .told</code> for an error variant. In a call expecting a one-argument function, <code>it</code> is the argument. Leave out what can be recovered; write what carries meaning. The <a href="reading.html">reading guide</a> has both.</p>
<example name="reading-target"/>

<h2 id="with">Copy-and-update</h2>
<p><code>a with { qty: 5 }</code> copies <code>a</code> with the listed fields replaced. It is the only copy-with-changes form; a type with <code>requires</code> checks the result again.</p>
<example name="reading-with"/>

<h2 id="except">except</h2>
<p><code>except</code> removes a pattern from a set. On an error set it narrows <code>fails</code>, so a caller sees exactly which errors remain; on a list or loop (<code>for x in xs except null</code>) it is planned.</p>
<example name="reading-except"/>

<h2 id="concurrency">Structured concurrency</h2>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> Tasks run inside a <code>scope</code> block with <code>spawn</code>. A task cannot outlive its scope: the scope waits for every child before exiting, and children may borrow the caller's stack. The first failure, a <code>timeout:</code> or <code>until:</code> deadline, or an outside cancel cancels the children, running their <code>defer failing</code> and <code>defer canceling</code> handlers:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">scope(timeout: 5s) {
    let aisha = spawn prepareIftar("Aisha")
    let bilal = spawn prepareIftar("Bilal")
    let mealA = try aisha.join()
    let mealB = try bilal.join()
}</code></pre>
<p>A scope can cap concurrent children with <code>limit:</code> or set a deadline with <code>timeout:</code>. Not implemented yet.</p>
